[August]a has been either the hometown or burial place of 10 Confederate generals — Brig. Gen.[ Edward] P.[ Alexander], Maj. Gen.[ Ambrose] R.[ Wright] and Gen. Mar[cell]us A. Stovall, among others. “When the South needed generals during the Civil War, it often turned to[ August]ans,[�]� the Augusta[ Chronicle] says. Today, you can see monuments all over the city that have been dedicated to these men.
The Masters wasn[�]�t always called the Masters; the title didn[�]�t come about until 1939. The tournament began in 1934 and was originally known as the Augusta National Invitational. That name didn[�]�t sound quite prestigious enough, however — so in 1939, the name was changed to the Masters.
